<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siward, Earl of Northumbria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1055">1055</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siward was an earl of Scandinavian origin who governed York under Cnut and his successors from the 1030s. In 1041 he killed Eadwulf of Bamburgh and brought all of Northumbria under his rule. In 1054 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and defeated Macbeth, a campaign remembered in Shakespeare's play. He died at York in 1055 and was succeeded by Tostig Godwinson.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siward-earl-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="Siward, Earl of Northumbria" width="320" height="121" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siward,_Earl_of_Northumbria_(British_Library_Cotton_MS_Tiberius_B_I,_folio_161v).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Siward was one of many Scandinavians who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after Cnut conquered most of the kingdom in the 1010s. Near-contemporary texts give him the Old Norse nickname Digri, rendered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Grossus, the stout. The Vita Waldevi, a hagiographic life of his son Waltheof, draws on legend to make him the son of an earl named Bjorn and descended from a polar bear. The historian Timothy Bolton has argued that Bjorn was probably a real man, a brother of Thorgil Sprakling, which would make Siward a first cousin of Earl Ulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p>The earliest certain record of him as earl is a charter of Cnut to Ælfric Puttoc, Archbishop of York, in 1033, in which he is styled dux. Robin Fleming called him &quot;the third man in Cnut's new triumvirate of earls&quot;, beside Godwine, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, and Leofwine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-mercia">Earl of Mercia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Siward married Ælfflæd, a daughter of Ealdred II of Bamburgh and granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uhtred-the-bold">Uhtred the Bold</a>; whether the marriage came before or after the killing of Eadwulf is unclear. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> he was one of the king's strongest supporters. On 16 November 1043 he marched with Edward and Earls Godwine and Leofric against Queen Emma, who lost her treasury, and in 1051 he raised forces for the king against the rebellion of Earl Godwine and his sons. Royal writs addressed to him suggest that he also gained the shires of Northampton in the 1040s and Huntingdon in the 1050s.</p><p>The campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> cost him his eldest son, Osbjorn. The battle was fought north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> and is known as the Battle of the Seven Sleepers; the tradition that it took place at Dunsinane goes back no further than Andrew of Wyntoun in the early 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry of Huntingdon told that Siward, struck by dysentery, put on armour and took up an axe and shield so as not to die like a cow, a story of doubtful historicity thought to come from a lost saga. He was buried in the church of St Olaf at Galmanho in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, which he is said to have built; it is the only recorded burial inside a church of a lay person who was not royal in pre-Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His surviving son Waltheof later became Earl of Northumbria, and through Waltheof's daughter, who married David I, King of the Scots, Siward was an ancestor of later Scottish and British monarchs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siward, Earl of Northumbria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Siward, Earl of Northumbria</b>, invading Scotland in support of Malcolm Canmore's claim to the throne, defeated Macbeth, King of Scotland, somewhere north of the Firth of Forth.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1041 <b>Earl Siward</b> killed Eadwulf, the last of the line, and united Northumbria under his own authority as part of the Kingdom of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-bamburgh">Earldom of Bamburgh</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=64.78,14.76&amp;year=1045" aria-label="Scandinavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4844 40 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Siward, Earl of Northumbria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5294 310h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5294 310h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=64.78,14.76&amp;year=1045">What was here in 1045? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
